= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <Hamiltonian vector field> convention $\iota_{X_{H_t}}\omega=-dH_t$. A smooth time-dependent <Hamiltonian function> $H:[0,1]\times M\to\mathbb R$ with flow defined over the whole interval generates the <Hamiltonian isotopy> determined by
$$
\frac d{dt}\phi_H^t=X_{H_t}\circ\phi_H^t,\qquad \phi_H^0=\mathrm{id}.
$$
The <group of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Ham}(M,\omega)=\{\phi_H^1:H\text{ generates such a flow}\}}.
$$
These maps are <symplectomorphisms>: by <Cartan's magic formula>, $\mathcal L_{X_{H_t}}\omega=d\iota_{X_{H_t}}\omega=0$. Thus $d(\phi_H^t)^*\omega/dt=0$.

The term “group” is justified explicitly. If $\phi_t,\psi_t$ are generated by $H_t,K_t$, their product path is generated by $H_t+K_t\circ\phi_t^{-1}$, since the pushforward of $X_{K_t}$ by a <symplectomorphism> is $X_{K_t\circ\phi_t^{-1}}$. The inverse path is generated by $-H_t\circ\phi_t$. The zero Hamiltonian gives the identity.

On a closed <symplectic manifold>, all these flows exist on the finite time interval. On an open manifold one often means the <compactly supported Hamiltonian diffeomorphism group> $\operatorname{Ham}_c$, using Hamiltonians with compact support in $[0,1]\times M$. We use that subgroup for the finite-energy definitions on Euclidean space below. The unrestricted complete-flow group is needed for the global cotangent translations in Question 4; those translations are generally not compactly supported.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $\phi$ in the <identity component> $\operatorname{Symp}_0(M,\omega)$ and choose a smooth <symplectic isotopy> $\phi_t$ from the identity to $\phi$. Let its generating <vector field> be $X_t=\dot\phi_t\circ\phi_t^{-1}$. Differentiating $\phi_t^*\omega=\omega$ gives
$$
0=\phi_t^*\mathcal L_{X_t}\omega=\phi_t^*d(\iota_{X_t}\omega).
$$
Thus $\eta_t=\iota_{X_t}\omega$ is a <closed differential one-form>. By the vanishing of the first <de Rham cohomology>, it is exact, so choose $H_t$ with $\eta_t=-dH_t$.

The primitives can be chosen smoothly in both $t$ and the point. On each connected component fix a base point $x_0$ and define $H_t(x)=-\int_{x_0}^x\eta_t$. Exactness makes this independent of the chosen path. In a coordinate neighbourhood of any point, use a fixed path to the neighbourhood's centre followed by a smoothly varying short path; this shows smooth dependence locally, and hence globally. One may subtract a time-dependent mean without changing the <Hamiltonian vector field>. Therefore the original <symplectic isotopy> is Hamiltonian, and $\phi\in\operatorname{Ham}(M,\omega)$.

Here the usual description of $\operatorname{Symp}_0$ by smooth isotopies agrees with the topological <identity component>. To see the local path-connectedness behind that description, use a <Weinstein neighborhood> of the diagonal in $(M\times M,-\omega\oplus\omega)$. The graph of a symplectomorphism close to the identity corresponds to the graph of a small closed one-form on the diagonal. Multiplying that one-form by $t\in[0,1]$ gives Lagrangian graphs; for sufficiently small forms their first projections remain diffeomorphisms, so they give symplectomorphisms connecting the original map to the identity. These local paths, translated in the group and concatenated with smooth time reparametrizations, identify its connected and smooth-path components.

Conversely every <Hamiltonian isotopy> is symplectic by part (a), and connects its time-one map to the identity. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Symp}_0(M,\omega)=\operatorname{Ham}(M,\omega)}.
$$

